# The problem

Writing a single PRD means scattering it across five tools. Google Docs for the prose, Sheets for the tables, Miro for the diagrams, Notion as the hub, Jira for the tasks it spawns.

The cost is not the writing. It is everything after.

- **Context is lost on the seams.** The diagram that explains the paragraph lives in a different tool from the paragraph.
- **Versions drift silently.** The board gets updated, the doc that embeds it does not, and nothing warns anyone.
- **Reconstruction is expensive.** Someone joining in month three cannot work out where anything lives, so they ask, and the cost lands on whoever wrote it.
- **There is no diff.** "What changed in this spec since last sprint?" has no cheap answer.

@note this is the whole bet

## Where the effort actually goes

@matrix x="Effort to keep current" y="How much it matters" style=boxed
  The decision log at top-left
  The diagrams at top-right
  Meeting notes at bottom-left
  The task board at bottom-right

The top-right quadrant is the problem. The diagrams matter most and cost most to keep current, which is exactly why they are the first thing to go stale.

@divider style=dots

## What makes this one different

Solving fragmentation alone gets you another all-in-one tool, and there are plenty. What makes this worth choosing is what comes out the other end.

@quote
  None of them treat the document as an artifact worth caring about.

@note the notebook is the differentiator, not the pitch

The bet is that documents people enjoy re-reading get re-read, and re-read documents keep teams aligned. Aesthetic is a retention feature, not decoration.

@decision title="Fragmentation leads, the notebook differentiates" status=accepted date=2026-09-18
  context: Two framings were live and contradicted each other. Tool fragmentation, and documents treated as freight nobody reopens.
  choice: Lead with fragmentation as the pain. Keep the notebook rendering as the reason to pick this over another all-in-one.
  consequences: Sets the landing headline, the README opening, and what the survey is actually testing.

The deciding argument was falsifiability. "Would you like nicer documents?" gets a yes from everyone and tells you nothing. "How many tools did your last PRD touch?" can come back and say the premise is wrong.

## How we find out

@timeline title="Validating the premise"
  Week 1: Survey opens
    30 PMs, one question that can fail
  Week 3: Interviews
    Five people who said five tools or more
  Week 5: Decide
    Premise holds, or the pitch changes

## The risk, held open

This still rests on a single-user hypothesis, which is the author's own pain. That is a legitimate origin and many tools started there, but it stays named rather than hidden until the survey returns data.

@panel accent=alert title="The risk is that the problem is not real"
  A single-user hypothesis is a starting point, not a finding. The survey is what turns it into one, or kills it.

@note a null result gets recorded, not buried
